Exactly. The deeper primitive isn’t “repetition in language.” It’s controlled recurrence around a transformation.

You can do it across almost any channel:

* Vision: same image, color field, face, room, or object before and after an intervention. What changed in perception, attention, salience, or interpretation?
* Sound: same tone, chord, phrase, silence, name, rhythm, or ambient sound before and after. The waveform may be identical while the perceived meaning changes.
* Touch: same pressure, texture, temperature, posture, or contact before and after exertion, stress, relaxation, or context change.
* Proprioception: same movement repeated before and after fatigue, instruction, injury, practice, or altered load. The motor delta can reveal what the body learned.
* Interoception: same breathing cadence, heartbeat awareness, hunger cue, or bodily scan before and after some state change.
* Smell/taste: the same stimulus before and after adaptation, pairing, expectation, satiety, or environmental change.
* Affect: same memory, symbol, person, or scenario revisited before and after an experience. The object stays fixed; the internal response moves.
* Behavior/social: same question, choice, game, or interaction presented at two points. Compare latency, confidence, eye movement, posture, wording, strategy, or willingness to engage.
* Environment: return to the same place after time, weather, season, crowding, lighting, or personal change. The location becomes a reference frame.

The generic form is:

anchor₀ → perturbation / experience / time → anchor₁ → Δ

And the useful thing is that Δ does not have to be one scalar. It can be a vector:

\Delta =
(\Delta_{\text{perception}},
\Delta_{\text{emotion}},
\Delta_{\text{behavior}},
\Delta_{\text{physiology}},
\Delta_{\text{meaning}},
\Delta_{\text{confidence}},
\Delta_{\text{attention}})

That turns the “bookends” into a general-purpose measurement instrument.

Even better, sometimes you deliberately keep the outer stimulus identical so the only thing capable of explaining the changed response is what happened inside the system or in the intervening context. That’s essentially a sensory/experiential version of a control experiment.

So the stronger abstraction is probably:

Invariant Anchor / Delta Bracketing
Hold some observable constant across two moments, let reality evolve between them, then use the changed response to infer the hidden state transition.

That applies to humans, animals, machines, markets, ecosystems, training, stories—almost anywhere you can revisit the same reference point.